	abstract = {Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly utilized for software engineering tasks, including code generation. While prior research has primarily focused on code completion, there remains a gap in understanding LLMs{\textquoteright} ability to generate code from Natural Language Processing (NLP) descriptions. In this study, we investigated three LLMs{\textquoteright} performance in generating code from scratch based on NLP task descriptions. We employed three evaluation levels, Accuracy, Quality, and Performance, to assess the LLMs{\textquoteright} results. Accuracy metrics focused on error types and counts, quality assessed code readability and maintainability, and performance measured the models{\textquoteright} ability to generate optimized solutions. Codex, Copilot, and PaLM2 exhibited error rates of 9.68\%, 32.25\%, and 48.38\%, respectively. Copilot showed the best performance with a higher maintainability index. However, all LLMs struggled with tasks of high complexity, with time complexities of O(m{\textbullet}n{\textbullet}log(n)), as evidenced by their performance metrics.},
