	isbn = {2375933X (ISSN)},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035189607?origin=resultslist},
	doi = {10.1109/BigComp68355.2026.00031},
	abstract = {Recent progress in large language models (LLMs) has reshaped the landscape of academic literature retrieval. However, most existing systems still rely on rigid, stage-wise pipelines with limited reasoning depth, making them ill-suited to capture evolving user intents or maintain precision during iterative exploration. We propose a multi-turn, tool-augmented agent that unifies clarification, retrieval, and filtering within a single decision loop. The agent learns the "clarify-retrieve-filter"process end-to-end, enabling dynamic refinement of search results through dialogue. We first employ an LLM-based user simulator to generate realistic multi-turn trajectories for supervised finetuning, and then introduce a Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) algorithm with process reward masking to address the long-horizon credit assignment problem. To support training and evaluation, we construct AnchorScholar, a 30k-instance dataset in which each sample uses an anchor paper's references as the ground-truth target set. Built on Qwen3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, and LLaMA3.1-8B, our agent consistently outperforms strong baselines - including Google, GPT-4o, PaSa-7B, PaperFinder, and SPAR - demonstrating superior adaptability and retrieval efficiency in multi-turn academic recommendation. {\textcopyright} 2026 IEEE.},
