	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035367691?origin=resultslist},
	doi = {10.1109/ISCEIC67854.2025.11405500},
	abstract = {The rapid advancement of Large Language Models (LLMs) has enabled unprecedented capabilities in natural language understanding, reasoning, and multi-step task planning. However, their integration with deterministic execution systems such as Robotic Process Automation (RPA) remains an open challenge, especially for complex workflows involving dynamic desktop environments and heterogeneous software tools. The system adopts a human-analogous architecture in which the LLM functions as the {\textquotedblleft}brain,{\textquotedblright} OCR as the {\textquotedblleft}eyes,{\textquotedblright} and RPA as the {\textquotedblleft}hands,{\textquotedblright} forming a closed-loop decision-action pipeline. The framework consists of three stages: (1) user intent understanding and structured task decomposition, (2) multi-agent collaborative execution across RPA, OCR, and LLM components, and (3) automated output packaging that generates standardized documentation and task-specific artifacts in multiple formats. Experimental evaluations on real-world desktop workflows demonstrate that the proposed architecture significantly improves task robustness, adaptability, and success rates compared with traditional RPA systems. This work contributes a practical blueprint for next-generation intelligent automation systems built upon foundation models. {\textcopyright} 2025 IEEE.},
